= WiiLand =

Status: final Rust 1.98 architecture, operations, and validation guide for one
Linux installation that supports Wayland and X.org sessions equally.

== Architecture ==

WiiLand is a display-neutral input stack with four layers:

1.  The kernel `hid-wiimote` driver and BlueZ expose the paired Wii Remote as
    input interfaces.  The native Rust `wiiland-hid` crate owns and decodes
    those interfaces, including core and extension buttons and axes,
    accelerometer, MotionPlus, IR, and Balance Board data.

2.  `wiilandd` consumes those interfaces and creates Linux uinput virtual
    evdev devices.  The gamepad profile creates one device named
    "WiiLand Virtual Controller" per remote.  The desktop profile creates a
    device named "WiiLand Virtual Desktop"; `both` creates both profiles.  The
    daemon does not call a display-server API and does not require XWayland.

3.  A Wayland compositor consumes the virtual evdev devices through libinput.
    An X.org server consumes the same virtual devices through its normal input
    stack.  On a Wayland desktop with XWayland available, the compositor still
    consumes the device and forwards the resulting seat input to XWayland
    clients.

4.  Applications consume the resulting seat or controller events through
    their normal interfaces: native Wayland applications, native X11
    applications, XWayland applications, SDL, Steam Input, and Wine/Proton.

uinput is the shared boundary because it is a kernel input interface, not a
display protocol.  One `wiilandd` instance can therefore publish identical
evdev devices to whichever session is active, without separate compositor
plugins or display-specific injection permissions.  Each session consumes the
same virtual devices through its own normal input stack; the session does not
change how the Wii Remote is decoded.

== Pairing and operating the bridge ==

Pair a remote with the desktop Bluetooth UI or the current `bluetoothctl`
command-line workflow.  Press the remote's sync button, then run `power on`,
`agent on`, `default-agent`, `scan on`, `pair <address>`, `trust <address>`,
`connect <address>`, `scan off`, and `quit` in `bluetoothctl`, replacing
`<address>` with the device shown by `scan on`.  The hid-wiimote driver must be
available; no kernel-driver configuration is needed.  `wiilandd --list --verbose`
shows connected devices, devtypes, and extension attributes without creating
virtual devices.

The daemon defaults to `backend=uinput` and `profile=gamepad`.  Use
`wiilandd --profile desktop` for pointer/keyboard output or
`wiilandd --profile both` for both virtual devices.  Desktop output maps valid
IR events and D-pad repeat to pointer motion; A/B click, +/- Enter/Escape,
Home overview, and 1/2 Page Down/Page Up.  Motion aiming is configured with
`aim-mode=mouse` or `aim-mode=right-stick`; `aim-source=auto` prefers IR,
then calibrated MotionPlus, then calibrated accelerometer tilt.

Applications may have remembered mappings for the former observable names
"WiiLand Wayland Controller" and "WiiLand Wayland Desktop".  The current names
are "WiiLand Virtual Controller" and "WiiLand Virtual Desktop"; select the new
devices or redo an application's mapping when necessary.  This is an
application configuration change, not a change to the evdev contract.

== Permissions and service lifecycle ==

`wiilandd` needs read access to the hid-wiimote `/dev/input/event*` nodes and
write access to `/dev/uinput`.  Install the udev rules
`res/70-udev-wiiland.rules` and `res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules` (or the packaged
equivalents).  The latter grants active-seat access to input nodes with
`TAG+="uaccess"` and retains `GROUP="input", MODE="0660"` as a fallback; the
former grants group write access to Wii Remote LED sysfs attributes.  No
display-server client access is needed.

The packaged user unit is `wiilandd.service` and is installed in the systemd
user-unit directory selected by `cargo xtask install`.  It is pulled by
`default.target`.  Its `ExecCondition` runs `wiilandd --check-config` before
`ExecStart`; invalid configuration therefore suppresses that start and does
not enter the runtime restart policy.  A process that starts and then fails
uses `Restart=on-failure` with `RestartSec=2s`.  Process-level hardening denies
privilege gain, personality changes, real-time scheduling, SUID/SGID changes,
and writable executable memory; it restricts syscall architectures to native
and uses `UMask=0077`.  Enable and start it in the user manager with:

    systemctl --user enable wiilandd.service
    systemctl --user start wiilandd.service

Use `systemctl --user status wiilandd.service` for lifecycle diagnostics.
`wiilandd --doctor` reports current session variables, Wayland/X11 and XWayland
availability, resolved configuration paths, `/dev/uinput`, backend, and
profile; it does not enumerate Wii devices.  `wiilandd --check-config`
validates the effective configuration without enumerating devices.

== Configuration and frontends ==

Unless `--no-config` is supplied, `wiilandd` reads the canonical system file
`/etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf` first, then an absolute
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf`, or
`$HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf` when `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is unset or
relative.  Later values override earlier values.  `--config path` selects one
explicit key=value file instead of the default hierarchy, and command-line
options override loaded values.  `--config` and `--no-config` are mutually
exclusive.  `--dump-config` prints the resolved result.  `res/wiilandd.conf`
is the installed/sample configuration; it is not an additional implicit layer.

The optional `wiiland-config` control center is a Rust eframe/egui application.
Its native winit backends support Wayland and X11, and its application ID is
`io.github.philosophimoonbeam.wiiland-config`.  The model and reducer preserve
canonical daemon values, transaction/revision ownership, atomic same-directory
saves, service controls, and stale asynchronous-completion protection.
Trace and calibration actions use the running daemon through IPC 1.1 and
are mutually exclusive; saved accelerometer and MotionPlus
calibration remain independent, with complete X/Y/Z triples for each enabled
sensor.  Fixed-width command output can be copied or cleared and retains the
most recent 10,000 text blocks.

The optional `wiiland-show` diagnostic is a ratatui/crossterm TUI.  It supports the
80x24 minimum and 160x48 extended layouts, full controller displays, and
terminal cleanup on exit. It uses daemon IPC by default, with q/f for quit and
freeze. Use `--direct` after stopping the daemon for LEDs, rumble, sensor toggles,
and normalization, or `--socket PATH` for a custom daemon endpoint. The GUI also
provides an explicit direct-hardware diagnostic mode. Capturing through IPC
keeps the daemon's normal virtual input active. `wiiland-dump` is a developer-only utility and is not
installed.

For processing boundaries, capture leases, and IPC 1.1 operations, see
`doc/ARCHITECTURE.md`.

== Build, installation, and artifacts ==

The workspace is pinned to Rust 1.98.0 by `rust-toolchain.toml` and uses Cargo
resolver 3, edition 2024, and package version 2.0.0.  Build optional
applications and integration assets with:
    cargo xtask build --release --features gui,tui,integrations

The xtask build compiles the selected Cargo workspace binaries and libraries
into the requested target profile.

The typed install workflow accepts logical GNU-style paths and stages them
with `--destdir`:

    cargo xtask install --release --destdir "$DESTDIR" \
      --prefix /usr --sysconfdir /etc \
      --features gui,tui,integrations

`--with-udev-rules-dir`, `--with-systemd-user-unit-dir`, and
`--with-xorg-conf-dir` independently accept `auto`, `no`, or an absolute
destination.  With integrations enabled, installation provides
`70-udev-wiiland.rules`, `70-wiiland-uinput.rules`, `wiilandd.service`, and
`50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf` in their selected system directories.  It installs
`wiilandd`, `wiilandd-hardware-report`, optional `wiiland-config` and
`wiiland-show`, `res/wiilandd.conf`, this documentation, and the applicable manual
pages.

The default logical prefix is `/usr/local`, while the system configuration
path remains `/etc`; `--sysconfdir` rejects any other path.  Distribution
packages normally pass `--prefix /usr --sysconfdir /etc` and a package-specific
`--destdir`.  `cargo xtask uninstall` uses the ownership marker for that staged
root and removes only files that still match the recorded content.

== X.org raw-device policy ==

For a native X.org installation, the X.org integration provides
`res/50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf` InputClass rules that suppress matching raw
Nintendo Wii Remote event nodes so X.org does not create duplicate pointer
input from accelerometer/IR axes.  The rules do not block `wiiland-hid` or
`wiilandd`, and do not suppress devices named "WiiLand Virtual Controller" or
"WiiLand Virtual Desktop".  The Classic Controller and Pro Controller
exceptions in that file remain enabled.  This policy has no role in a Wayland
session.

== Reproducible validation ==

This guide records the acceptance procedure; no physical Wii Remote hardware
validation is implied by this source tree or by an execution without paired
hardware.  First perform deterministic, hardware-free checks:

    wiilandd --self-test
    wiilandd --check-config
    wiilandd --dump-config
    wiilandd --axis-map
    wiilandd --validation-checklist
    wiilandd --doctor
    wiilandd-hardware-report

The report records schema version, host and UTC time, distribution, session
type/compositor, Bluetooth controller, `hid-wiimote` parameters, permissions,
wiilandd version, commit/dirty state, resolved config, axis map, checklist,
doctor diagnostics, device list, devtype, extension, sysfs identity, battery,
event-node access, and manual-validation fields. It exits with a failure status
for core diagnostic failures; manual fields remain explicit acceptance fields.

With a paired device, use the device number from `--list` or its sysfs path:

    wiilandd-hardware-report N
    wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=motion-plus
    wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=ir

These commands perform dry-run, verbose traces and do not create virtual
devices.  Exercise every button, stick, trigger, accelerometer, MotionPlus
axis, IR source, and attached extension.  After traces are correct, stop the
user service if it is running and create live devices with:

    wiilandd --device N --profile both

Alternatively, set `profile=both` in the effective configuration and restart
the user service. Record the following paired matrix. Each row records kernel
and wiilandd versions, distribution, Bluetooth adapter, devtype, extension
state, session, consumer, expected result, and pass/fail details.

Hardware coverage:

  - original Wii Remote: core buttons, accelerometer, and IR desktop pointer;
  - original Wii Remote with external MotionPlus: extension hotplug and X/Y/Z;
  - Wii Remote Plus with built-in MotionPlus: core, accelerometer, and X/Y/Z;
  - Nunchuk: stick, C/Z buttons, and accelerometer passthrough;
  - Classic Controller: sticks, triggers, and face buttons;
  - Wii U Pro Controller: sticks, triggers, and face buttons;
  - Guitar: fret buttons, strum bar, whammy, and stick;
  - Drums: pad hits, cymbal/tom pressure, bass, and hi-hat pedals;
  - Balance Board: all four weight sensors.

Session and consumer coverage for each applicable hardware/profile pair:

  - native Wayland compositor: gamepad and desktop profiles through libinput;
  - native X.org session: virtual devices through X.org, with the raw-node
    InputClass policy installed and verified not to suppress virtual names;
  - XWayland application in a Wayland session;
  - SDL input tester;
  - one Steam Input game, including `aim-mode=right-stick`;
  - one native Wayland or XWayland non-Steam game;
  - one Wine/Proton game;
  - desktop pointer/buttons with `aim-mode=mouse` where applicable.

For motion validation, run `--calibrate-aim` on a flat, still remote, put its
generated key=value lines in the test configuration, and record any optional
IR screen bounds.  Mark a row only after observing the expected virtual
device/events in that session and consumer; do not substitute a report field
for paired hardware evidence.

== Archival references ==

Protocol history and device captures remain available in `doc/PROTOCOL`,
`doc/DEVICES`, `doc/DEV_REMOTE`, `doc/eeprom.dump`, and `doc/rdesc.dump`.
They document the hardware and wire-level lineage; the Rust implementation and
the Linux `hid-wiimote` interface are authoritative for current behavior.
